From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anthoine Bourgeois <anthoine.bourgeois@vates.tech>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:37:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vhd-util: coalesce: merge whole blocks into the parent

Coalescing called vhd_io_write() on the parent for every contiguous run
of sectors of the child. Each call reads and writes the parent bitmap,
scans the whole BAT twice to find the end of data, rewrites the footer
twice and truncates the file, and rewrites the whole batmap whenever a
block becomes full: O(blocks^2) work on large disks, and a dozen
synchronous I/Os for a fragmented 4 KiB run.

Merge one block at a time instead:
- read the child bitmap, then the span of data covering all its set
  sectors, in at most two reads;
- for an unallocated parent block (dynamic parent with the same block
  size), allocate it at a cached end of data and write the alignment
  gap, the bitmap, the data (holes zeroed) and the trailing footer in a
  single write, then write the modified BAT sector. With -s, holes of a
  partial block are still left unwritten;
- for an allocated parent block, write the runs, then read, merge and
  write the parent bitmap once, only if it changed;
- set batmap bits in memory and write the batmap and the footer once at
  the end, even on error.

Data and bitmaps reach the disk before the BAT entry pointing to them,
and an unset batmap bit only costs a bitmap lookup, so an interrupted
coalesce leaves a consistent parent. Raw and fixed parents write the
runs with pwrite(); a dynamic parent with a different block size keeps
going through vhd_io_write(). Progress is only printed when it changes.

On a 2 GiB chain, the parent contents are identical to the previous
implementation and vhd-util check passes; write syscalls drop from
97030 to 12238 for a child of fragmented blocks and from 2725 to 815
for a child of full blocks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
